The canonicalisation in this example is a copy
----------------------------------------------
The two fingerprints of a message have exactly one right answer, so this example
does not reimplement the way they are computed: the files under
`vendor/canonical/` are a copy of the ZEKNOP reference implementation of the
canonical content specification, taken from one fixed revision of it and
carrying this licence header in place of the one the source file carries: the
same licence and the same copyright holder, and in addition what the file is a
copy of. Apart from that header and the substitutions recorded with the copy —
each of them inside a comment, and none of them changing the program — nothing
in those files was changed.

The copied files:

    vendor/canonical/base.mjs
    vendor/canonical/digest.mjs
    vendor/canonical/envelope.mjs
    vendor/canonical/free-form.mjs
    vendor/canonical/headers.mjs
    vendor/canonical/index.mjs
    vendor/canonical/message.mjs
    vendor/canonical/mime.mjs
    vendor/canonical/multi-byte.mjs
    vendor/canonical/sig-alg.mjs
    vendor/canonical/single-byte.mjs

The substitutions are the ones a published file calls for: a comment that
pointed at a file of the publisher's own repository now points at the section of
the specification that states the same rule, and a comment written about one
particular reader is written about any reader of this protocol. That the code
itself is the same program as the code of that revision was checked when the copy
was made, by parsing the source and the copy and comparing both their lists of
tokens and their parse trees.

`single-byte.mjs` and `multi-byte.mjs` are generated from the index data of the
WHATWG Encoding Standard (https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/), and `free-form.mjs`
from pinned Unicode character data and the HTML5 named character references; each
of those files names its own sources in its header, and `free-form.mjs` also
carries the pinned Unicode version in its code.

Where a comment in the copied files says something was measured — a time, a
memory peak, how two readers disagreed — the measurement is the publisher's,
made while the reference implementation was written, and its evidence is not
part of this package. Those numbers say why a line of code has the shape it has;
they are not a claim about how fast this example is. Where a comment says a
property is held by a test or a gate, that test is the publisher's too.

That the copy answers what the reference implementation answers is not a promise
made here: the `conformance` command runs it over every vector the preview
publishes, and `../../vectors/CONFORMANCE.md` says what a matching report means and
what it does not.

Everything else in this directory is written for this example.
